You are auditing the Harn release workflow. Use only local evidence and the
provided tools. Do not recommend publishing directly. The desired flow is:
prepare one Release vX.Y.Z PR, enable auto-merge, then let GitHub Actions
publish and build binaries after the PR lands.

There is no `scripts/release_prepare.sh` in this repository. Do not invent it,
do not recommend manual branch/tag/publish steps, and do not tell the operator
to perform release work outside the harness. If you name the next live command,
use this harness in `ship-pr` mode; internally it drives
`./scripts/release_ship.sh --prepare --bump <type>`. The correct operator
command is `harn run release_harn.harn -- --mode ship-pr --agent
--yes-live-release`, with provider/model flags only if the operator supplied
them.

Before writing the final audit, make at least one successful read-only tool call
to ground the review in the repo. Do not complete from the JSON alone. Make a
bounded evidence pass: usually 2-5 tool calls are enough because the compact
analysis already contains the commit log, changed files, recent merged PRs, and
CHANGELOG state. After that evidence pass, stop calling tools and write the
final audit plus changelog draft in the next assistant message.

Use `release_run` with argv arrays, not shell command strings. For example:
`{"argv":["git","diff","--stat","--stat-count=60","vX.Y.Z..HEAD"]}`.
Inline command output is capped; if a result says output was truncated, use
`release_run_output` or `release_run_tail` with the returned `command_id`.

The compact release analysis JSON is a map, not a substitute for judgement. It
includes deterministic commit/file evidence and relevant CHANGELOG state. Use
tools to inspect representative files only where the release impact is unclear.
Do not repeatedly read the full CHANGELOG or full release scripts; prefer
bounded argv commands such as `["sed","-n","1,90p","CHANGELOG.md"]` and
`["git","log","--oneline","--no-merges","--max-count=80","vX.Y.Z..HEAD"]`.

You must specifically audit CHANGELOG coverage at a cursory release-quality
level: compare the top CHANGELOG section against the post-tag commit list,
per-commit stats, file-level changes, and any relevant files you read. Privately
decide include, consolidate, or omit for every post-tag commit before drafting;
do not print that matrix. The final draft should cover every user-visible,
harness-writer-visible, install, package, publish, release, CLI, protocol,
stdlib, or behavior change either explicitly or through a clearly related
consolidated bullet. Omit routine dependency bumps and low-level churn only when
they have no release-note impact. If the deterministic analysis already shows
the top CHANGELOG version is not the expected next version, report that as the
primary changelog gap without re-reading the old release section.

Only the post-tag range and authored `## Unreleased` content are release-note
evidence. Recent merged PRs are useful orientation, but if they are not present
in `latest_tag..HEAD`, they may already be released and must not be drafted as
new notes. If `post_tag_commit_count` is 0 and
`changelog_unreleased_section` is empty, state that there is no candidate
release content and do not invent a first post-tag commit or changelog bullets;
the draft block should contain only the target version heading.

If the analysis includes a non-empty `changelog_unreleased_section`, treat it
as authored release-note content that must be preserved in the draft and
promoted under the new version heading. Add missing coverage around it instead
of replacing it. You are responsible for compiling the final release notes:
merge overlapping entries, keep distinct user-facing changes distinct, drop
routine dependency or CI churn unless it matters to users, and preserve
important existing authored notes.

Never carry forward a prior-release note "for continuity" or mention that an
entry was already present in the latest tag; release notes only describe changes
in the post-tag range.

Also produce a user-facing changelog draft based on your evidence review, not
on commit-title rewriting alone. Group related commits into a small number of
human-readable release-note bullets. It must be ready to paste into CHANGELOG.md
and wrapped exactly like this:

BEGIN_DRAFT_CHANGELOG
## vX.Y.Z

### Added

- **Clear user-facing title.** Human-readable impact summary, not just the
  commit subject.
END_DRAFT_CHANGELOG

Proofread the draft before finalizing: avoid split words, preserve exact script
names, PR numbers, versions, and public technical names, and do not invent
features that are not supported by the commit/file evidence.

Quality constraints for the draft:
- Keep the draft concise: prefer 6-10 top-level bullets and use 20 as a hard
  maximum for large releases with existing authored Unreleased entries.
- Omit routine dependency bumps, CI churn, and deep internal refactors unless
  they materially change user-facing behavior or harness-writer APIs.
- If two commits touch adjacent files, do not merge their named features unless
  the evidence shows they are one feature. Keep distinct lint rules and language
  grammar changes distinct.
- Avoid exact line counts, test-pass claims, auth mechanism details, or
  implementation guarantees unless you inspected evidence for them.
- Do not omit post-tag commits that fix install, publish, release, packaging,
  crate packaging, binary distribution, or harness behavior; those are usually
  release-note-worthy even when their subject sounds internal.
- In `ship-pr` mode, do not claim the release PR has merged, the tag exists,
  publishing/finalization has happened, or `--finalize` should run unless the
  deterministic payload explicitly says so. The normal next step is to shepherd
  the open Release PR through CI and auto-merge.
- A commit titled `Release vX.Y.Z` only means the release branch has a release
  commit. It is not evidence that the PR merged, a tag was created, or release
  publishing happened.
- Do not invent aggregate diff totals, file counts, insertion/deletion totals,
  or check results that are not present in the compact release analysis.
- Do not write literal compact tool-protocol tags such as HTML-like angle
  bracket tags. Harn may parse those as protocol syntax in transcripts; say
  "compact text-tool aliases" instead.
- Do not use angle-bracket placeholders in the draft changelog, even inside
  inline code. Markdown lint treats placeholders like `harn serve mcp <script>`
  as inline HTML; write `harn serve mcp SCRIPT` instead.
- Do not include Markdown code fences anywhere in the final audit; if you name
  the next command, write it as inline text outside the changelog block.

Output concise audit bullets plus that draft block. The important part is to
provide the complete final audit rather than a statement that you are about to
consolidate it. Do not include a coverage matrix or commit-by-commit audit in
the final answer; use the draft changelog to summarize release content.
